From b6f544631f4b29035cc8dc47c32d365f970b7104 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:54:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10209/11362] Prevent capstone milestone rot; record the
 untracked supervisor arm

TWO ITEMS, both step-6 conversions of lessons this session paid for.

CAPSTONE ROT LINT. A capstone freezes MILESTONE at authoring time; two of them
then read the LIVE research-roadmap.yaml and raised unless it still matched, so
they were green only during their own milestone. One built its artifact at
MODULE scope, so the raise landed at COLLECTION and pytest abandoned the entire
run -- 57,917 tests interrupted by one file, and every conductor task shelling
out to `pytest tests/python` failed with it. The correct pattern already existed
23 times (compare the ARTIFACT's own milestone). Capstones are planner-emitted,
so there is no generator to fix; a lint is the only place the lesson survives.

NARROWED TWICE, AND THE FALSE POSITIVES ARE THE INTERESTING PART. Matching
`payload[...] != MILESTONE` by variable name flagged SIX correct capstones whose
validators also name their argument `payload`. Matching any comparison flagged
EIGHT more that merely RECORD `"milestone_matches": ... == MILESTONE` as a
field -- reporting a mismatch is honest, refusing on it is the rot. Either
version would have shipped a check that cries wolf, which CLAUDE.md rightly
calls worse than the gap it closes. The rule is now: ONE function both reads the
live roadmap (by literal or by module constant -- the offenders used the
constant, and a first AST version missed them entirely and was clean-but-blind)
AND raises or asserts on the mismatch.

Verified both directions: catches 2 of 2 pre-fix offenders recovered from git,
clean across all 133 live capstone modules. 6 tests, 5 of 5 mutations RED.

A GIT-RECOVERY EXEMPTION WAS WRITTEN AND THEN DELETED. Its mutation came back
GREEN -- structurally unreachable, because a recovery helper raises at function
level rather than inside an `if ... != MILESTONE`. Deleted rather than kept with
a test built around it: a rule whose removal changes nothing protects nothing.
The assert branch failed the same mutation for a DIFFERENT reason -- reachable
but untested -- so it got a fixture instead. Both outcomes are in the tests.

SUPERVISOR ARM. reset_after_stagnant_repeat is in neither ARM_ORDER nor the
REQ-6720 ledger, yet it is the only arm with live evidence: 9 firings, all on
vc33, benefit_observed False in 9 of 9, utility delta -0.333, 0 wins. The
retirement rule is `fired >= floor and helped == 0` with floor 10, so it
satisfies one half and sits ONE firing short -- and being untracked, those 9
were never counted and never will be. Recorded, not acted on: retiring on n=9
with a CI touching zero is the over-reading the floor exists to prevent.

